#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676} :activity/id #uuid "045805f2-d7d9-501d-8e4b-541e2bb65e50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315 :topic/id #uuid "93e6a7ce-2e63-5dea-bbbb-a00575db5770" :topic/math-academy-id 1852} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "05c77385-415b-5833-b935-9be3a2038c69" :step/math-academy-id 17469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119619 :question/id #uuid "d5a81b39-421e-55c3-90de-d9f4e6489cd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4795"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaefbc53-9df9-54a0-a969-66e062553d5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421530}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "74f8be2d-0f86-52de-8277-fd7db395c80d" :step/math-academy-id 17471 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121036 :tutorial/content "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/dd/ddef437718d3a11811da02a17e2207d75e2d1852eeada67ab494af45b2a655e2.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/1f/1f92378ff1d1409f0928caa4b3ce98861784d0f2aa51dd1eda30e8877a0134ff.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3194 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span"} :step/id #uuid "15713f7b-cd11-55c3-93fd-8040ac62d36f" :step/math-academy-id 17477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119620 :question/id #uuid "94a17888-dcef-58e0-8147-0dec3745f136" :question/math-academy-id "e-4799"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050308}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421528}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"} :step/id #uuid "33abdb88-235d-550f-b5bb-06946454437c" :step/math-academy-id 17478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119671 :question/id #uuid "21a6808a-5b1c-5b1d-9bd4-da605fb6d920" :question/math-academy-id "e-6218"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fac58a79-2d3f-5ad3-b1ab-c5ce279c4ebe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421532}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Such That a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "5cca50af-2a48-5696-bc3a-fc57e95232b0" :step/math-academy-id 23264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120678}}] :activity/title "Linear Span of Vectors in N-Dimensional Euclidean Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120046}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158117} :activity/id #uuid "93123186-af04-50ed-9f1a-970323c9acfd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048681 :topic/id #uuid "b45261a2-56f4-554a-ba13-6946f5dc3f8c" :topic/math-academy-id 2828} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157088 :question/id #uuid "35acfa60-3395-5f11-94f9-627499386932" :question/math-academy-id "e-7159"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a9b6de-b0dd-5b38-a32b-378d2b1d86a2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368561}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Difference of Sets Without a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "ca424c53-0556-5e18-9bc0-d39fdd10d96b" :step/math-academy-id 26359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158114}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157089 :question/id #uuid "89c94a89-21de-5a4f-a365-85701b2e4ef2" :question/math-academy-id "e-7160"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e90c94-5018-582f-8692-a1155f41f7c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368798}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Differences of Sets Given Verbally"} :step/id #uuid "94c5e547-ed98-5f00-a17f-fb87e8db7939" :step/math-academy-id 26360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158474 :tutorial/content "The **difference** of two sets $A$ and $B,$ denoted by $A \\setminus B$ or $A - B,$ is the set of all elements belonging to $A$ and *not* belonging to $B.$\n\nFor example, consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/4a/4a2d3be8c64b5527dcb37ac68d99cd1e36009725d0847597c5bbd417bbd5f7ad.png)\n\nThe difference $A \\setminus B$ consists of all the elements that are in $A$ but not $B.$ To represent $A \\setminus B,$ we will shade in all the area that is contained within $A$ but not $B.$\n\n![](images/49/49743d4647817294c2287bc15a496fb5d6c73070ed52cfd8934d1babe93c1b25.png)\n\nThe elements $a$ and $c$ are contained in $A$ but not $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ a,c \\}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The difference can be defined using set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ x \\mid x \\in A \\text { and } x \\notin B \\}\n$$\n\nThe condition \"$x \\in A \\text { and } x \\notin B$\" means that $x$ must belong to $A$ but $x$ must *not* belong to $B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "48529a2b-cc59-57e8-8624-78fd4339f88c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4333 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e4b9023-ad62-55c8-950f-2d1e0371a65e" :step/math-academy-id 26364 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157093 :question/id #uuid "9e0d2770-a884-558f-becb-a38fe709b2a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7167"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4687dcd7-a5ab-5dc6-be6d-89d806a61a02" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354498}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Difference of Sets Using a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "46adbc13-f6f2-55a5-861d-63519ad07f7b" :step/math-academy-id 26372 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158480 :tutorial/content "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize differences, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a difference.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ This time, we will compute the difference $A \\setminus B$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements that are in $A$ but not $B.$\n\nSo, we will go through the elements of $A$ and check if each element is also in $B.$ Remember that we are looking for elements that are *not* in $B.$\n\n- For the element $a \\in A,$ we have $a \\not\\in B. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $b \\in A,$ we have $b \\in B. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $c \\in A,$ we have $c \\not\\in B. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nThe elements of $A$ that are not in $B$ are $a$ and $c.$\n\nTherefore, the difference is\n\n$$\nA \\setminus B = A - B = \\{ a,c \\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "14afef0e-fec6-5cdf-82c4-98391973df49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4342 :tutorial/title "Differences Without Venn Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "2ef8179b-8279-55d0-b781-5afd8ba23248" :step/math-academy-id 26397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158113}}] :activity/title "The Difference of Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157485}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125368} :activity/id #uuid "ca8227e6-ef47-5702-8c4f-321ab31e8355" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729 :topic/id #uuid "b90e497c-df74-56b9-a5d7-98c7bdb922cb" :topic/math-academy-id 1920} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125899 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a straight line passing through the point $P(3,-1,7)$ and that's parallel to the direction vector ![](images/54/54405c5c6e2502e5174525c2ee36a99366a79933b47e316a09858f827fda8dae.svg) As we know, we can write the vector equation of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nUsing the vector equation above, we can create a set of **parametric equations** where we express a general point $(x,y,z)$ in terms of  the parameter $t.$\n\nIf we let ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) be the position vector of the general point $(x,y,z),$ then the above equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1+5t \\\\ 7-3t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n![](images/64/64cf325014eff341673557321ef565e35988b836630f3fbeb52af8024d7aaad7.svg)\n\nThe equations in the above system are called the **parametric equations** of the straight line.\nGeometrically, the equation states that a point belongs to the line if and only if its coordinates have the form\n\n$$\n(3, \\: -1+5t, \\: 7-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n![](images/e2/e29ce95cdccb47b97578f6431a96e608f7af9f6709410b221ff517b436896608.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ff73cc8-91a0-5a25-9c14-6e87496adac8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "de4015ef-1002-54f7-adee-e5eee4e87979" :step/math-academy-id 189149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124498 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124227 :question/id #uuid "001af492-25b1-5f75-b030-668708eee118" :question/math-academy-id "e-15602"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f232aed9-1584-5e57-a881-226a6876384a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400296}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Straight Line Given Parametrically"} :step/id #uuid "1c0d5ba6-1e60-5d4d-b42e-60ba32609db6" :step/math-academy-id 189150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124499 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124228 :question/id #uuid "7a6038c2-3f7b-5dfa-a9e2-256b246fcc20" :question/math-academy-id "e-15603"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3699391-68e8-5c59-8ca2-83fded9f22de" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398298}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Parallel To a Straight Line Given Parametrically"} :step/id #uuid "b5810a9c-9d11-54cc-9534-f5807fd71ff5" :step/math-academy-id 189153 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124500 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124229 :question/id #uuid "173b99e8-d00c-57c8-995e-ba198361480e" :question/math-academy-id "e-15604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "debd4ccc-de40-55a6-9236-9bf9fff0bdb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302398}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Line"} :step/id #uuid "83088d79-842b-5477-8d05-56eb6c96e451" :step/math-academy-id 189156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124501 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124230 :question/id #uuid "a9c13555-9d30-5dfc-be5a-13f0597c2ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-15605"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d275a218-aa12-54bd-a4ff-4be892226f4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290485}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "5a457aae-70d6-5725-8620-6593b8838a70" :step/math-academy-id 189159}] :activity/title "The Parametric Equations of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124717}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707} :activity/id #uuid "28cf8e1a-d07a-556d-a006-2d022a4740c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850 :topic/id #uuid "c60f74c0-bf82-529d-b9dd-b8bfe8e2cc4a" :topic/math-academy-id 1799} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121048 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b2a904a-eda3-58e1-9b0f-e742454f48b6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3245 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "762cbf4d-a393-51df-98bb-da96c034848b" :step/math-academy-id 17885 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119637 :question/id #uuid "005f8f3f-89eb-5e99-bf0f-82b67329661a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4910"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048729}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413273}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given in Canonical Form"} :step/id #uuid "0e305d64-a5b0-5e37-85c9-271d9bd1785b" :step/math-academy-id 17886 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119638 :question/id #uuid "897aa930-02ca-5f79-95d0-5ee11420da97" :question/math-academy-id "e-4911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413270}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given in Parametric or Vector Form"} :step/id #uuid "15d988ae-4b19-5d9f-a7e2-fd6fd8c3f4c6" :step/math-academy-id 17887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119639 :question/id #uuid "df314d1d-56c8-5503-b632-8a425b47070f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413267}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line Given Two Points on the Line"} :step/id #uuid "1e9d9f49-2eb3-5745-9da4-70a87dddd9f6" :step/math-academy-id 17888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121055 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n![](images/9d/9d4a6d56170de2e0dbfa7e2a6f51905b19f9ada9fa1900aafe2672f54831be86.svg)\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0bea89e098af6be643450df08d30b97c0c3d658075b0b891ab2c4c7f37077f42.svg)\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n![](images/f3/f399dae55ae8c720f7769fa78a42b30b2a7b7aab49ed1515d9c43d371f3a9f71.svg)\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a2d263d-c94f-5d86-859a-c6c79c835eca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3316 :tutorial/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "c618a49e-3866-523c-b010-33f9b474436e" :step/math-academy-id 18457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120729 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119656 :question/id #uuid "74fb2af1-d791-5687-98cc-0b2db5bd8464" :question/math-academy-id "e-5021"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413272}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero"} :step/id #uuid "669d1115-ca36-5052-810d-cb802269d1fa" :step/math-academy-id 18458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120710}}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120015}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172765} :activity/id #uuid "5ec40baf-865b-5a44-991d-3e7fe88c0d58" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049243 :topic/id #uuid "e61e45e7-7c85-53b1-babf-321815b4e8ac" :topic/math-academy-id 3404} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171956 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171808 :question/id #uuid "104ad420-9a16-51b9-bfc5-16080b22fd49" :question/math-academy-id "e-5108"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be11193b-a241-5812-a1c1-54d233ae9ddd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335994}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Binomial Coefficient"} :step/id #uuid "2865273f-2c72-526a-8ae4-5f9b2067a41a" :step/math-academy-id 18724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172764}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171957 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171809 :question/id #uuid "587f0594-4911-5ad7-9f1a-bd33cfbad3fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5109"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c976b6a-4523-51e1-a243-27aa8937db25" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335998}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Relationship Between the Rows of Pascal's Triangle in Terms of Binomial Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "1189fea7-65d2-5d2d-87d5-800d8204673c" :step/math-academy-id 18731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173144 :tutorial/content "Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (on the left) is position number $0.$\n\nWe can write a value in Pascal's triangle as $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}},$ where the value is located in row $\\color{red}a$ and position ${\\color{blue}b}.$\nThis is known as a **binomial coefficient**.\nIn words, we say $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{a} \\choose {\\color{blue}b}}$ as \"$\\color{red}{a}$ choose ${\\color{blue}b}$\".\n\nFor example, the value located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle{{\\color{red}4} \\choose \\color{blue}{2}} = 6.$ Remember, we start counting from zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "62d4b142-a86a-52c9-b45a-1153afedf865" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3393 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "133af8ac-b2ce-5603-9334-ce3da1e8d068" :step/math-academy-id 18977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171810 :question/id #uuid "9f9efb07-33a0-5a28-8eef-656a3c00a200" :question/math-academy-id "e-5231"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5c108c0-f32a-5725-af3b-c3e747da09f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049341}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338779}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Binomial Coefficients Using the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "0b7576cb-372f-5a6a-ad5b-4f0b958a39e3" :step/math-academy-id 19269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173146 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wanted to calculate the value of $\\displaystyle{30 \\choose 3}.$ If we were to use Pascal's triangle, we'd have to write out $31$ rows, which would take a long time. Thankfully, there is a formula for calculating binomial coefficients. \n\nThe binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b89d1a7e-10c1-5ec2-b745-c3c45e0d7814" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3893 :tutorial/title "A Formula for the Binomial Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "b2f96998-bbd7-586b-b0d8-4cdbe2c42034" :step/math-academy-id 23007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172766}}] :activity/title "Pascal's Triangle and the Binomial Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172193}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158119} :activity/id #uuid "eda4b8d4-8ce0-5365-bd61-2bc0a26d9eab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049691 :topic/id #uuid "0b3f5fae-90b4-5349-943c-fbbbfb27afe4" :topic/math-academy-id 2829} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157286 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157090 :question/id #uuid "7bd73f85-c4d5-57c4-a3ba-094d93c105cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7163"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19fb3196-439e-5466-b50c-615c492167b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354480}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complement of a Set Using a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "0e1053ea-8f35-5110-850e-9f25114d0ab8" :step/math-academy-id 26366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158475 :tutorial/content "Remember that a set is a collection of objects. The set containing *all* objects that we can consider is called the **universal set**.\n\nFor example, if the universal set is $U = \\{ a, b, c, d \\},$ then the objects that we can consider are the letters $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n- One set we can consider is $\\{ a, b, d \\}.$ We consider this set because all of its elements are taken from the universal set. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- One that we *cannot* consider is $\\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}.$ We do *not* consider this set because its elements are *not* taken from the universal set. $\\quad\\color{red}\\times$\n\n- Likewise, we cannot consider the set $\\{ c, d, e\\}$ because one of its elements, $e,$ is *not* taken from the universal set. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$" :tutorial/id #uuid "08cce8fa-a37d-53dd-a7fa-3aae49e7a4a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4334 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "33879739-abba-55f3-b8a5-d084db3d9a64" :step/math-academy-id 26367 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157287 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157091 :question/id #uuid "1c497253-5007-52a4-9f93-838d4b4699b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7164"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a55a708-abb0-5e23-8862-153561d022c8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354481}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complement of a Set Without a Venn Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "de1f4b1a-c890-5d63-97f2-67f09441b0b3" :step/math-academy-id 26368 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157288 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157092 :question/id #uuid "2647b940-84c6-5b52-9c99-6a4ee5ebb669" :question/math-academy-id "e-7165"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49251196-c06b-5718-82e6-5ed9450301af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048400}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382736}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complement of a Set Given Using Ellipsis Notation"} :step/id #uuid "9f5dd11c-55e7-5927-a8da-bb00351bd1bf" :step/math-academy-id 26369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158476 :tutorial/content "If $S$ is a set in the universe $U,$ then the **complement** of $S,$ denoted $\\overline{S},$ consists of every element in $U$ that is **not** in the set $S.$\n\nFor example, consider the set $S = \\{ 2, 5\\}$ in the universe $U = \\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}.$ We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/20/20726b35619c1b04803c18c8fe12f52db8f07faebca45f64a86c17f8f7077d09.png)\n\nThe complement of $S,$ denoted $\\overline S,$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $S.$ To represent $\\overline S,$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $S,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c7/c7b25f22a1f70135d09886db58be3646604a861e62d34978a3563343fcd7b298.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $1,$ $3,$ and $4.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $S.$ Therefore, the complement of $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{S} = \\{ 1,3,4 \\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that there are several ways to denote the complement of a set. The complement of a set $S$ can be denoted as $\\overline{S},$ or $S^c,$ or $S'.$ Here, we will use the convention $\\overline{S}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e80b18cd-6881-563b-a21e-aca78742ce9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4336 :tutorial/title "The Complement of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "854a2440-ad18-5582-be3b-b439bdd69c76" :step/math-academy-id 26377 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158477 :tutorial/content "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize complements, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a complement.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the set $S = \\{ 2, 5\\}$ in the universe $U = \\{ 1,2,3,4,5 \\}.$ This time, we will compute the complement $\\overline S$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements of $U$ that are not in $S.$\n\nSo, we go through the elements of $U$ and check if they are in $S,$ as follows. Remember that we are looking for elements that are *not* in $S.$\n\n- For the element $1 \\in U,$ we have $1 \\not \\in S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $2 \\in U,$ we have $2 \\in S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $3 \\in U,$ we have $3 \\not \\in S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $4 \\in U,$ we have $4 \\not \\in S. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $5 \\in U,$ we have $5 \\in S. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nWe found that the elements $1,3,4$ are in $U$ but not $S.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\overline{S} = \\{ 1,3,4 \\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6966558b-99a7-527c-b2bd-fe2f9b3a7d1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4337 :tutorial/title "Complements Without Venn Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "f4d0f842-3087-5f70-9224-231463b1ace3" :step/math-academy-id 26378 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158120}}] :activity/title "Set Complements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157486}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134946} :activity/id #uuid "f84989a7-0989-5902-8883-a3b0d6c6b258" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049863 :topic/id #uuid "19611553-019e-5d8b-8bd9-3fd48d54d619" :topic/math-academy-id 2162} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134946 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135067 :tutorial/content "Let's use the sign table method to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-2)(x-3) \\leq 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) =(x+2)(x-2)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nNext, we find the solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0,$ which are $x=-2, x=2,$ and $x=3.$ Since the inequality is not strict, these values *are* solutions to the inequality.\nMoreover, these roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n\\left( -\\infty,-2 \\right), \\qquad (-2,2), \\qquad (2,3),  \\qquad (3,\\infty)\n$$\n\nOn each of these intervals, the factors of the polynomial are either always positive or always negative. We can find out which by filling a sign table:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $x-2$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $x-3$ |  |  |  |  |\n| $f(x)$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTo fill out the sign table, we have to figure out the sign of each factor on each of the intervals. We can do this by finding the sign of each factor at a chosen test point in each of the intervals:\n\nIn the interval $(-\\infty, -2),$ we can choose the test point $x_1=-3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-3+2=-1<0, \\\\ x-2 & =-3-2=-5<0, \\\\ x-3 & =-3-3=-6<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso all three factors are negative $({\\color{red}-})$ on the interval $(-\\infty, -2).$\n\nBy applying the same method for the other intervals and placing the signs in the sign table, we get the following table.\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nTo fill in the final row, we compute the product of the signs in each of the columns. This gives the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,3)$ | $(3,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nTo solve the inequality $f(x)\\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(2,3).$\n\nFinally, since this is an \"or equal to\" inequality, the roots of $f(x),$ namely $x=-2, x=2,$ and $x=3$ are also solutions to the inequality. Therefore, we have that $f(x) \\leq 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2] \\cup [2,3].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4a0ddc80-ade5-5065-a8d2-05cbd39daf8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3761 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e54d5d51-d705-5cc5-aa8f-17ebc4e1cf25" :step/math-academy-id 463954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133623 :question/id #uuid "2aca2611-5903-5a92-8409-8fe3af32614a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5827"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c80b75e7-19b2-534b-97f8-aba563ebe058" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050039}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357446}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Cubic Polynomial Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "b49b29fd-0657-5b99-808e-a8ba692d3a3d" :step/math-academy-id 463955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134948}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134948 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133666 :question/id #uuid "cc631d97-d7f8-5798-8dff-f816e63bbf44" :question/math-academy-id "e-7755"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e675f1c-9093-5f6e-a3b3-112d0bc8e516" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357447}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Factored Higher-Order Polynomial Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "a21cdc05-785b-5273-802a-0ecf07149dd6" :step/math-academy-id 463958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133938 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133624 :question/id #uuid "2cdfee85-e9c7-5e91-bbef-16daaaaf1d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-5828"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050827}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326866}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Expanded Polynomial Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "1dba4cbb-7316-55e8-ac0c-1a2638ad2499" :step/math-academy-id 463961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134950}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133626 :question/id #uuid "bad47d1a-817e-529d-8eb5-7a2ab446370a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5835"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295246}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Polynomial Inequality when the Right-Hand Side is Nonzero"} :step/id #uuid "76d4486b-98cf-5094-be17-7add2fc6bbff" :step/math-academy-id 463964}] :activity/title "Solving Polynomial Inequalities Using the Sign Table Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134024}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055877} :activity/id #uuid "16bd6886-0670-51ea-9c62-c70c7e015ce4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088 :topic/id #uuid "2c984580-e283-5888-86e7-f792567f1a8a" :topic/math-academy-id 55} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056003 :tutorial/content "Interesting mathematical problems often involve a large (or infinite) collection of sets. Labeling each set with a subscript is often convenient in such situations. \n\nFor example, suppose we define a collection of sets $A_i$ for $i\\in\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA_1 = \\{1,2\\}, \\qquad A_2 = \\{3,4\\}, \\qquad A_3 = \\{5,6\\}, \\qquad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This collection of $A_i$'s, which we denote $\\big\\{ A_i \\big\\}_{i \\in \\mathbb N},$ is called a **family of indexed sets.**\n\n- The set $i=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$ is called the **index set**.\n\nWriting each set with a subscript makes talking about a general $A_i$ easier. In this particular case, for example, we can express a general member of this family in terms of its index:\n\n$$\nA_i = \\{2i-1,\\, 2i\\}, \\qquad i\\in\\mathbb N\n$$\n\nSo, if we wanted to write down the elements of $A_{100},$ we substitute $i=100$ into the definition of $A_i{:}$\n\n$$\nA_{100} = \\big\\{2(100)-1,\\, 2(100)\\big\\} = \\{199,200\\}\n$$\n\nWe can use any index set when defining a family of indexed sets. \n\nFor example, consider the indexed family $\\big\\{ B_x \\big\\}_{x \\in \\mathbb R},$ where $B_x$ is the real interval defined by\n\n$$\nB_x = \\big[ 0, x^2 \\big), \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nHere, the index set is $\\mathbb{R}.$ \n\nTo write down, say, the elements of $B_{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}},$ we substitute $x={\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}}$ into our definition of $B_x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}_{\\sqrt{5}} & =[0,\\,(\\sqrt{5})^{2}) \\\\  & =[0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94ce30ce-17fc-5813-9947-89a8d392343b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7957 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9a6cdf07-394b-572b-b1c9-fc2d60400ed0" :step/math-academy-id 70625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054362 :question/id #uuid "154d4cf7-a72a-51b1-bbb3-9bcf83b88e98" :question/math-academy-id "e-12665"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f986c29-111e-5cb3-8104-62ab734f1c47" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284706}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of an Indexed Family"} :step/id #uuid "0b2b9b72-b07b-51b4-970f-a98643e205c5" :step/math-academy-id 70626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056033 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the indexed family $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ where $I$ is an index set. \n\nThe **union** of a family of indexed sets is the set containing all elements belonging to *at least one* set in the indexed family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{ -i, \\,0, \\, i \\}, \\qquad i\\in \\mathbb N\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the following union:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our union, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $X_1 = \\{ -1, 0, 1 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $X_2 = \\{ -2, 0, 2 \\}.$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $X_3 = \\{ -3, 0, 3 \\}.$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on. \n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- It's clear that the $X_i$'s contain only integers. Therefore, any union of $X_i$'s will contain only integers. So, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\subseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- Notice that *any* integer $n$ belongs to our union. For instance, $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ lies in $X_n=\\{ -n, 0, n \\}$ and, consequently, lies in the union of all $X_i$'s. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in \\mathbb N} X_i  \\supseteq \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\nThus, using the fact that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$ if and only if $A=B,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i\\in N}{X}_{i} & =\\overset{{X}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-1,0,1}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-2,0,2}}}\\cup \\overset{{X}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{{-3,0,3}}}\\cup ⋯=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋃}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{X}_{i}=Z\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44240f43-3346-5c5f-9955-317e4e8ad24c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8177 :tutorial/title "Unions of Indexed  Families"} :step/id #uuid "2ec1fad8-799b-53d9-878f-444b40b1e568" :step/math-academy-id 70630 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056034 :tutorial/content "Similarly, the **intersection** of a family of indexed sets $\\{A_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{ x\\in U \\: : \\: x \\in A_i \\:\\:\\textrm{for all } A_i,\\:\\: i \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following family of indexed sets:\n\n$$\nS_i = \\left[ 0, \\: 10^{-i} \\right], \\qquad i \\in \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the following intersection:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in \\mathbb{N}} S_i\n$$\n\nTo calculate our intersection, let's write down a few of our indexed sets:\n\n- If $i=1,$ we have $S_1 = [0, 0.1]$\n\n- If $i=2,$ we have $S_2 = \\left[ 0, 0.01 \\right]$\n\n- If $i=3,$ we have $S_3 = \\left[ 0, 0.001 \\right]$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nand so on.\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- As $i$ increases, the right endpoint of $S_i$ approaches $0$ but never reaches it.\n\n- The left endpoint equals zero for each $S_i.$\n\nSo, the only element that lies in all sets is $0.$ Therefore, the intersection of our indexed family is the set containing zero only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}={0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since our index set $I = \\mathbb N$ in this example, we can also use the following notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i=1}^{\\infty }{S}_{i}={0}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03cd6876-c452-576d-8f1a-24bb0fe077ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8178 :tutorial/title "Intersections of Indexed  Families"} :step/id #uuid "253c7481-555b-5d32-a3f7-49642ec94a10" :step/math-academy-id 70631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054369 :question/id #uuid "60e24dac-c47a-53ee-9c91-0e6987e966f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "957a2f61-61b1-5675-ba84-5348a47af986" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287881}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions and Intersections of Finite Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "c74f24a2-dcab-503c-9e0c-8b67b381e9c4" :step/math-academy-id 70632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054370 :question/id #uuid "842ee2c1-8e78-56dd-b109-b9fcc273704e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12764"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e93acb-6c0b-5263-9df9-9dcc00680845" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363555}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unions and Intersections of (Countably) Infinite Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "36a1848e-f2f5-52f9-9aad-05995f22fb49" :step/math-academy-id 70636}] :activity/title "Indexed Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054911}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116106} :activity/id #uuid "c73bd47d-c95f-5782-913d-11a66b5cf13b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463 :topic/id #uuid "4bb17014-060c-5398-ac8a-30c6798d15e9" :topic/math-academy-id 1729} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116490 :tutorial/content "Matrices can be used to represent systems of equations, such as the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y=5 \\\\ 4x\\text{ }-\\text{ }5y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\times 2$ system like the one above, we put the coefficients of the system into a $2\\times 2$ matrix, called the **coefficient matrix**:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEach row has the coefficients of one of the equations in the system, and each column consists of the coefficients of one particular variable.\nWe also have the $2\\times 1$ column vectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}$ consists of each constant on the right side of the system and $\\mathbf{x}$ contains the variables.\nWith these matrices, we can write our system as $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ or\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can write any system of two equations with two variables in this form.\nJust to check that the above equation is true, let's multiply out the left-hand side to see if it equals the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 4x\\text{ }-\\text{ }5y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =b\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d49da49a-c3f8-5718-91a9-4adc236155d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3048 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f7a59c72-2c98-5267-942e-326053020128" :step/math-academy-id 16463 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115062 :question/id #uuid "87bf279f-9f78-54cb-b9c3-e1bf1c5426ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-4505"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce2e21aa-f89d-5d10-b815-c66a2b46ef5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354631}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a 2x2 System of Equations Using a Matrix Product"} :step/id #uuid "b1ea43d1-62f0-5ea5-b504-4e92139c4783" :step/math-academy-id 16464 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115232 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115063 :question/id #uuid "359f4b72-60d2-54e2-b7ef-c48f1e3af708" :question/math-academy-id "e-4506"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4f746a1-ea9e-5136-90d6-d4dee1306c17" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299619}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a 2x2 System of Equations Using a Matrix Product by Reorganizing"} :step/id #uuid "8395e7bd-de36-5136-8556-30a9d038a483" :step/math-academy-id 16465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115233 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115064 :question/id #uuid "b87aa5eb-f689-5f34-baeb-f97c3f200552" :question/math-academy-id "e-4507"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2300a1-f31e-5a24-889a-6f45e1e8647b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403495}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Matrix Product as a 2x2 System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "896b2689-9de5-527f-a925-513c7675ce5c" :step/math-academy-id 16466}] :activity/title "Representing 2x2 Systems of Equations Using a Matrix Product" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115475}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111588} :activity/id #uuid "2bf966de-3fac-57c5-99d5-21cc8c224f16" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050641 :topic/id #uuid "5b7cc53f-24b7-5a92-abb0-e272bef77223" :topic/math-academy-id 1535} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110651 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110535 :question/id #uuid "ae48dbff-25c8-55a9-a9e0-fda0b3ecfd50" :question/math-academy-id "e-7959"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbd52408-5f2f-58c9-9a88-bd992fe8752c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050153}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404468}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Strict Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "c956bed0-e7a0-55c2-ba2f-0eeac3a7e01f" :step/math-academy-id 29676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111580}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110536 :question/id #uuid "4809beec-7c4e-5bd1-989d-1dc54d78d892" :question/math-academy-id "e-7960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a258070e-5c3c-595e-81d6-3e0f9b9a5daf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404475}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Mixed Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "420d1396-a598-5166-9063-80e51d94ab6d" :step/math-academy-id 29678 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110537 :question/id #uuid "88a7aab5-813a-58e2-a695-8ee72dd4efb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049646}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404461}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "2acd7fcc-ee79-517d-a38d-0a1aeeaa4fda" :step/math-academy-id 29702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111822 :tutorial/content "Just as we can have a system of linear equations, we can also have systems of linear inequalities. For example, the system\n\n![](images/42/422ff3fd1ff0704d34d5d2f098e3c524f409d2a4da7d6fa3d792020bbc8ac56d.svg)\nis solved by the points that satisfy the inequalities ![](images/23/23f2bda2195f51e1cbf95f6905d547dc9004733d89866ae9f92ef046c330417c.svg) *and* ![](images/87/877dcbf886336f3054b3df8910cee950868e2819e007cf11544a6be7f15b81d3.svg) simultaneously.\n\nTo find the points that satisfy both inequalities, we first draw the graph of the inequality ![](images/9a/9a758452dbe8e0ede75fb97296110356c18e46275000ac28ebf631d91fa05b22.svg)\n\n![](images/6f/6f3f7b6eb3109225256c5f4342333d03c19ef31b40d5ae5b24c136950a31f76a.png)\n\nNow, on top of the diagram above, we draw the graph of the second inequality, ![](images/c1/c1e37a6573ce7b419aeff619c181b43325304505c05b669394ef0818ace2555f.svg)\n\n![](images/e0/e017eefa235ac4b95009570c59fe5e5587f50bb02299305cfb9126573c0156bd.png)\n\nThe solution to the system is the *intersection* of the two regions (i.e., the part that is shaded twice).\n\n![](images/c1/c1d9b4358e15ca09e8358f8e298fc0ffa745b885adcd7ea522e07f1d221a2c90.png)\n\nThe important thing to realize is that *any* point in the shaded region above satisfies *both* inequalities." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e005009-6033-5e6b-b50c-c802bbffe3e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1473 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f5b482d0-1628-52e2-8a01-cbff575355b1" :step/math-academy-id 3482 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110290 :question/id #uuid "4b19b389-7d0e-525b-b37f-787e53f5e3b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1056"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cd1bb61-2770-5096-83d6-d1e4a7189e9a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050095}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403037}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing \"Or Equal To\" Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "712b9963-4a02-5cba-8c6c-337db89072ce" :step/math-academy-id 3483 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111579}}] :activity/title "Systems of Linear Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110879}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675} :activity/id #uuid "5052a9d6-d9c5-5df7-993c-818d53b162fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765 :topic/id #uuid "64dbabd7-08e6-5435-b006-ab4997312f47" :topic/math-academy-id 148} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060938 :tutorial/content "We can use matrices to solve systems of linear equations. To do this, we need to set up the **augmented matrix** for the system.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the system of equations below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=-1 \\\\ -3x+7y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the **coefficient matrix**, which consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we create the **augmented matrix**. This is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the future, we will learn how to use coefficient and augmented matrices to solve systems of equations. But for now, let's practice setting them up." :tutorial/id #uuid "36b1a271-77b9-59ed-9f12-1b6675c26830" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3146 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5b2031d1-6747-5c9a-b8e2-711f6dd1e094" :step/math-academy-id 17169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059576 :question/id #uuid "b2196eb1-6691-549b-950a-a9bab6863a70" :question/math-academy-id "e-4683"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb13e9d9-19f0-5f39-b9f8-e6e2f8fac433" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338975}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "8e2bc116-2181-5120-9389-ff1ba9b3ee5e" :step/math-academy-id 17170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060677 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059577 :question/id #uuid "b5967dd6-bdf9-5cd0-9639-ec2816d04256" :question/math-academy-id "e-4684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9ccaa5-d69c-584d-946e-0c57b3050160" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338976}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "e6584e81-18d3-5c82-b510-c6d84d369be3" :step/math-academy-id 17171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060939 :tutorial/content "We will use mostly systems with the following variables:\n\n- $x,y,z,$ or\n\n- $x_1,x_2,x_3,$ or\n\n- $a,b,c.$\n\nThese sets of variables will always have a *natural* order:\n\n- alphabetical order in the case of $x,y,z$ or $a,b,c,$ and\n\n- numerical order in the case of $x_1, x_2, x_3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f6081e68-9014-5a0d-a6bf-084abe5fd28e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3147 :tutorial/title "The Order of the Variables in a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "17bd5ecf-ddf0-5755-a7bc-19c589337468" :step/math-academy-id 17172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060676}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059578 :question/id #uuid "05942b5f-c165-59c6-8b67-24bc8dd40cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-4685"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c8f4da-c782-51e0-ac51-87a89f31860d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409515}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the System of Equations Associated With an Augmented Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "9c9a13d7-cb68-5563-a0cd-305ad4cad7f7" :step/math-academy-id 17173 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059579 :question/id #uuid "161a4327-2356-5779-92a6-8495cfffefb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4686"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14d047c8-f032-5ff3-aced-65d40bfeeaad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338981}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a Linear System With Variables Other Than x, y, z"} :step/id #uuid "f6cdbf1b-5a64-5b3d-b66b-3ba956756491" :step/math-academy-id 17174}] :activity/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059882}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055553} :activity/id #uuid "8a7c3e16-7b5a-517e-b02a-29c35f81f008" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056044 :tutorial/content "Given two sets $A$ and $B,$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we say that $A$ is a **subset** of $B,$ and we write \n\n$$\nA \\subseteq B.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ {\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{3}} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1,2,3\\}, \\qquad C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ef02f03-d232-5ab1-88fc-1f363535caa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8240 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4c7c6c6-5b36-53d1-a4de-4c76a981a433" :step/math-academy-id 152757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056055 :tutorial/content "We note the following points regarding subsets:\n\n- Every set $A$ is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\nA\\subseteq A\n$$\n\nThis is because $A$ contains the same elements as $A,$ and therefore $A$ is a subset of $A.$\n\n- The empty set is a subset of *every* set. So, for all sets $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq A.\n$$\n\nTo understand this, remember that $\\emptyset \\not\\subseteq A$ means that $\\emptyset$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $A.$ However, this cannot be true since $\\emptyset$ contains no elements! Thus, we must have $\\emptyset\\subseteq A.$\n\nIt's worth pointing out that the empty set is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFinally, note that a set $A$ is a **proper subset** of a set $B$ if $A \\subseteq B$ and $A \\neq B.$ In this case, we can write \n\n$$\nA \\subset B.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}, \\qquad C = \\{ 3,2,1 \\}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a proper subset of $B,$ but $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B{:}$\n\n- Since every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we have that $A\\subseteq B.$ Also, $A\\neq B.$ Therefore, $A$ is a *proper* subset of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nA\\subset B\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $C\\subseteq B$ because every element of $C$ is an element of $B.$ However, $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B$ because $C=B.$ Therefore, we write \n\n$$\nC \\not\\subset B.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1acb16ee-d59d-5050-9848-9cfdb6e2ee6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8316 :tutorial/title "Some Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "9cee0391-700f-550d-9b6e-139dae503e1e" :step/math-academy-id 152758 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054442 :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395707}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "b812cc2f-347b-5ed3-979d-e5bff1beb134" :step/math-academy-id 152759 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056047 :tutorial/content "We can systematically list all the subsets of a given set using a tree diagram.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the subsets of the set $A=\\{ 0,1 \\}.$\n\nWe list every possible combination of elements within the set using a tree diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd51f5e02c9f280b8478e11497602dd7ae886b84d1d5994d8af73dff9719d20e.png)\n\nWe start with the empty set as the root $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}}.$ \n\nNext, we consider the element $0\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset.\n\n- If we don't include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we are left with the empty set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we get the set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}.$\n\nFinally, the list of all subsets is shown in the last column:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "daebe910-6059-595b-a45a-fc890a9b1b53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8244 :tutorial/title "Listing All the Subsets of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "547755c4-e17d-56bf-a674-91a455f0dbbf" :step/math-academy-id 152763 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054443 :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408073}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "2e361262-f159-54e6-a942-659d5017ac7b" :step/math-academy-id 152764 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056056 :tutorial/content "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$\n\nFrom these basic inclusions, we can now deduce some subset relationships for other special numerical sets. For example, recall that [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] represents the set of all non-negative integers. \n\n* Obviously, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}[/math] since all the elements of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] are integers but there are some integers, say, [math]-1,[/math] that does not belong to [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]\n\n* On the other hand, [math]\\mathbb{N}[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] since all natural number lie in [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] but [math]0 \\notin \\mathbb{N}.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math]\n\nTherefore, we have [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "c336106d-52a5-5d56-a98f-c64cd26eef4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8317 :tutorial/title "Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5386697a-6e4f-5619-83d7-53f1064c31f2" :step/math-academy-id 152767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054444 :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399131}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "053e7255-8298-5305-9c62-d263e5b3b5ba" :step/math-academy-id 152768 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056073 :tutorial/content "The notation \n\n$$\nA \\supseteq B\n$$\n\nreads \"$A$ is a **superset** of $B$\", and is equivalent to $B \\subseteq A.$ This is similar to how we can write either $x \\leq y$ or $y \\geq x$. The two notations mean the same thing. \n\nSimilarly, $A \\supset B$ reads \"$A$ is a **proper superset** of $B$,\" and is equivalent to $B \\subset A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d87be2be-dac5-52e2-bcbd-7c0ad0d17032" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9065 :tutorial/title "Superset Notation"} :step/id #uuid "d545e0c5-338a-5d32-8901-ef36ab148f6e" :step/math-academy-id 152771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054445 :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351173}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"} :step/id #uuid "7b56c42d-9c31-515e-ae69-cd2b8675b68c" :step/math-academy-id 152772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056075 :tutorial/content "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n> *Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> S = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n> $$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nThe issue, unknown to mathematicians until later, is that the object [math]S[/math] is not a set. If it were, Russell's paradox would not occur since we should always be able to determine whether an object is a member of a set or not.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set. \n\n* If [math]S\\notin S,[/math] then by the definition of [math]S[/math] and applying De Morgan's law for conjunctions, we have\n\\begin{align*}\nS\\notin S \\Leftrightarrow \\lnot (S\\subseteq U\\land  S\\notin S)  \\equiv (S\\not\\subseteq U) \\lor (S\\in S).\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e2d16c6-3382-5ba2-8d4b-949482eb4f07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9388 :tutorial/title "Russell's Paradox: Why We Need a Universal Set"} :step/id #uuid "7280da1b-4410-550f-9a00-d46456526bfc" :step/math-academy-id 164820}] :activity/title "Subsets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054909}]}
